  ✗ REJECTED: Manipulable Uniswap spot tick controls option solvency and l
    Reason: Upon reviewing PanopticPool._validateSolvency, liquidate, 
forceExercise, and the oracle helpers in PanopticMath, I determined that the 
alleged spot-tick vulnerability is mitigated by the implemented design. Solvency
valuation uses fast and slow observed oracle ticks, not the instantaneous tick: 
_validateSolvency computes median observed prices and evaluates collateral at 
those oracle ticks. Liquidation additionally obtains getUniV3TWAP(), requires 
the account to be insolvent at that TWAP, and rejects execution when the 
pre-liquidation slot0 tick differs by more than MAX_TWAP_DELTA_LIQUIDATION (513 
ticks). The current tick is used primarily for fee/premium accounting and for 
post-burn execution economics, while liquidation collateral requirements and the
principal bonus calculation use the TWAP. Although liquidate uses finalTick for 
token-denomination conversions and certain premium paths use currentTick, these 
values are bounded or applied after the TWAP insolvency gate and do not allow an
attacker to make a solvent account liquidatable through a temporary price 
excursion. The public calculatePortfolioValue function accepts an arbitrary tick
but is view-only and does not affect protocol state or solvency.


